1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,270
Imagine pointing a

2
00:00:01,310 --> 00:00:03,320
powerful laser at the moon.

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:05,060
Not to light it up.

4
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:06,870
To ask it a question.

5
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:09,380
You fire a pulse at a reflector

6
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,390
sitting on the lunar surface.

7
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:13,310
Now, how long before

8
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:15,120
that light comes home?

9
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:17,490
About two and a half seconds.

10
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:19,010
Wait, that's there and back.

11
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:21,470
Not two and a half seconds each way.

12
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:23,710
A little over a second to reach the moon

13
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,830
and a little over a second to return.

14
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:27,360
And because the moon's

15
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:28,840
distance keeps changing,

16
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,570
the timing changes too.

17
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:31,930
But hold on.

18
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,420
Why would the light come back at all?

19
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,390
Did someone leave a mirror on the moon?

20
00:00:38,210 --> 00:00:38,990
Actually,

21
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:39,900
yes.

22
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:41,630
Apollo astronauts left special

23
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:44,040
reflectors on the lunar surface.

24
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:46,280
Their corners have a clever trick.

25
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:48,610
They redirect a tiny fraction of incoming

26
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:51,040
light back toward where it came from.

27
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:52,900
Back on Earth, a telescope

28
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,270
looks for the return.

29
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:57,070
And here's the really strange part.

30
00:00:57,550 --> 00:00:58,620
Almost none of the light

31
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:00,400
makes it back to the detector.

32
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:02,790
Scientists have to collect just a few

33
00:01:02,830 --> 00:01:05,680
returning photons from many laser pulses.

34
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:07,460
A few photons.

35
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:08,770
They even account for the

36
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:10,350
effects of Earth's atmosphere.

37
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:12,390
And somehow,

38
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:14,000
those incredibly faint returns

39
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:15,500
let them measure the distance

40
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:17,340
with remarkable precision.

41
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:19,530
So how does it work?

42
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:21,470
A cosmic stopwatch.

43
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:23,140
Take the round trip time,

44
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:24,910
multiply it by the speed of light,

45
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:26,290
then divide by two.

46
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:27,990
That's your distance.

47
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:31,530
The moon isn't just hanging in the sky.

48
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:33,600
With each faint return,

49
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:35,460
we can measure where it is.

